{
 "format": 1,
 "book": "influence",
 "title": "Influence",
 "author": "Robert Cialdini",
 "year": 1984,
 "language": "en",
 "words": 2229,
 "minutes": 15,
 "parts": [
  {
   "title": "Click, whirr",
   "text": [
    "Robert Cialdini begins with a confession: all his life he had been a patsy, the sort of person who ends up buying magazines he does not want and tickets to events he will not attend. As a psychologist at Arizona State University he studied why people say yes in the laboratory, and then, for nearly three years, he went undercover among the people who get yes for a living. He answered newspaper ads for sales trainees, learned to sell encyclopedias, vacuum cleaners, portrait photographs and dance lessons, and studied advertising and fund-raising from the inside.",
    "What he found was a handful of principles that work because they usually serve us well. His first example is a friend who owned a jewellery shop in Arizona and could not sell a case of turquoise. Leaving town, she scribbled a note telling her head saleswoman to mark everything in the case at half price. The saleswoman misread it and doubled the prices, and the turquoise sold out. Tourists who knew little about turquoise were using a simple rule: expensive means good.",
    "Animals run on such rules all the time. A mother turkey cares for her chicks when they make a cheep-cheep sound and ignores them when they do not. In one experiment she even mothered a stuffed polecat, her natural enemy, as long as it played a recording of that sound. Cialdini calls these automatic responses click, whirr: a trigger is pressed and a whole sequence of behaviour plays out.",
    "People have their own. In a study by Ellen Langer, a woman asked to cut into a queue for a copying machine. When she said she had five pages and asked to go first because she was in a rush, 94 per cent let her. With no reason, 60 per cent did. When her reason was empty, because she had to make some copies, 93 per cent still agreed. The word because did most of the work. These shortcuts save us endless effort, and anyone who knows the trigger can press it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "The debt we feel",
   "text": [
    "The first principle is reciprocation: we feel obliged to repay what another person has given us. Cialdini calls it one of the most widespread rules of human culture. In 1985 Ethiopia, in the middle of a famine, sent 5,000 dollars of aid to Mexico after an earthquake there. The reason was that Mexico had helped Ethiopia when Italy invaded it in 1935, fifty years earlier.",
    "Because the obligation is so strong, a small unwanted gift can produce a much larger return. Members of the Hare Krishna movement raised large sums in airports by pressing a flower into a traveller's hand, refusing to take it back, and then asking for a donation. Many travellers threw the flower in the nearest bin after paying; the Krishnas collected them and handed them out again. In an experiment by Dennis Regan, people who had been given a free soft drink by a stranger later bought twice as many raffle tickets from him as people given nothing, and they did so whether or not they liked him.",
    "The rule also works through concessions. A request refused, followed by a smaller one, feels like a favour that deserves a favour in return. Cialdini was once stopped by a boy selling five-dollar tickets to a circus. When he declined, the boy asked whether he would buy some one-dollar chocolate bars instead. Cialdini bought two, though he does not like chocolate. In his own experiment, students asked to take a group of young offenders to the zoo for a day agreed 17 per cent of the time. When they were first asked to volunteer two hours a week for two years, and refused, 50 per cent agreed to the zoo trip.",
    "The defence Cialdini suggests is to accept gifts and concessions politely, but to redefine them for what they are once they turn out to be sales tactics. A trick does not create a debt."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Staying true to what we said",
   "text": [
    "The second principle is commitment and consistency. Once we have taken a stand, we feel pressure to act in line with it, and we come to believe it more. People at a racetrack, for example, were more confident of their horse's chances just after placing a bet than just before.",
    "In one experiment on a New York beach, a researcher spread a blanket near a stranger, listened to a radio for a while, and walked off. Another researcher then grabbed the radio and ran. Only 4 people out of 20 tried to stop him. But when the owner had first asked the neighbour to watch his things, 19 out of 20 gave chase, some of them physically. A small commitment had turned bystanders into guards.",
    "Small commitments can lead to large ones. Researchers asked homeowners in California to put a huge, ugly sign reading DRIVE CAREFULLY in their front gardens, and 17 per cent agreed. Among homeowners who had agreed two weeks earlier to show a tiny sign about safe driving, 76 per cent agreed. The first yes had changed how they saw themselves: as people who care about such things. The Chinese used the same method on American prisoners during the Korean War, starting with requests for mild statements, such as that the United States is not perfect, then asking prisoners to read and expand them. Written commitments worked best, because they could be shown to others.",
    "The pressure is strong enough to support many sales tricks. Toy companies advertise a few toys heavily before Christmas and ship too few of them. Parents who promised one buy other toys instead, and then, when the advertising returns in January, buy the promised toy as well. Car dealers offer a low price, let the buyer commit, then remove the advantage, and most buyers go through with the purchase. Cialdini's defence is to listen to the sinking feeling in the stomach that says a commitment is being used against you, and to ask whether, knowing what you know now, you would make the same choice again."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Doing what others do",
   "text": [
    "The third principle is social proof: when unsure what to do, we look at what people around us are doing. Television producers add canned laughter because audiences laugh more at jokes followed by it, even when they know it is fake and the jokes are weak. Bartenders put a few notes in the tip jar at the start of the evening, so that tipping looks like the done thing.",
    "Social proof works most strongly under uncertainty, and that can be deadly. Cialdini describes the experiments of Bibb Latané and John Darley on bystanders. In one, a student who seemed to be having a seizure got help 85 per cent of the time when one other person heard it, and 31 per cent of the time when five others were present. Each person looked to the others for a cue, saw everyone else doing nothing, and concluded that nothing needed doing.",
    "His advice for an emergency follows directly: do not appeal to a crowd. Pick one person, look at them, point, and give an instruction: you, in the blue jacket, call an ambulance. Once one person acts, others tend to follow.",
    "The pull is strongest when the others are like us. The sociologist David Phillips found that after a suicide was reported on the front page, suicides rose in the area where the story ran, and so did car crashes and plane crashes, some of them disguised suicides. The rise was greatest among people similar to the person in the story. Cialdini's defence is to notice when the evidence of the crowd has been faked, as with laugh tracks and staged testimonials, and to look up from the crowd now and then to check the facts for ourselves."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Saying yes to people we like",
   "text": [
    "The fourth principle is liking. We prefer to say yes to people we know and like, and the people who make a living from yes know it. At a Tupperware party, the demonstrator sells, but the guests are buying partly from their friend the hostess, who gets a share of the sales. Joe Girard, whom the Guinness book named the world's greatest car salesman, sent every one of his past customers a greeting card each month that said simply that he liked them.",
    "Several things make us like people, and each can be used. Physical attractiveness works like a halo: in Canadian federal elections in 1974, attractive candidates received more than two and a half times as many votes as unattractive ones, while voters denied that looks had influenced them. We like people who resemble us, even in small things such as clothing, which is why salespeople are trained to look for similarities. We like people who compliment us, even when we can tell the praise is not true.",
    "Contact helps when it is pleasant, and cooperation helps most. In a famous experiment at a boys' summer camp, Muzafer Sherif turned two groups of boys into bitter rivals through competition. Contact alone did not reduce the hostility. What did was a series of problems that the groups could solve only by working together.",
    "We are also influenced by association. People blame weather forecasters for bad weather, as though the messenger had caused the message. Sports fans say we won after a victory and they lost after a defeat; Cialdini found that university students wore their school's clothing more often after the team had won. His defence against the whole principle is simple: when you find you like someone more than you would expect after a short acquaintance, separate the person from the deal, and judge the deal on its merits."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Obeying the uniform",
   "text": [
    "The fifth principle is authority. We are trained from childhood to defer to legitimate experts, and usually this makes sense. But the deference can become automatic. In Stanley Milgram's experiments, about two thirds of ordinary volunteers kept giving what they believed were painful electric shocks to a protesting man, up to the highest level, because a researcher in a grey lab coat told them to continue.",
    "Doctors carry this kind of authority. In one study, a researcher telephoned hospital nurses, introduced himself as a doctor they did not know, and ordered them to give a patient twice the maximum dose of a drug that was not on the approved list. In 95 per cent of cases the nurse went to give it before being stopped. Cialdini also tells of a doctor who ordered ear drops for a patient's right ear, abbreviated on the prescription as R ear. The nurse put the drops in the patient's rectum, and neither she nor the patient questioned it.",
    "Often the symbols of authority are enough without the substance. Robert Young, an actor who played a doctor on television, sold large amounts of coffee in advertisements by advising people, as a doctor might, to drink a decaffeinated brand. Titles and clothes are enough to trigger deference. In one study, people were three and a half times more likely to follow a man crossing the street against the light when he wore a suit and tie than when he wore work clothes.",
    "The defence Cialdini proposes is two questions. Is this authority really an expert on the matter in hand, or only dressed like one? And how truthful can we expect this expert to be here, given what they gain? A salesperson who admits a small drawback in a product before praising it is often using honesty as a tactic."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Wanting what we might lose",
   "text": [
    "The sixth principle is scarcity. Opportunities seem more valuable when they are less available, and the thought of losing something moves us more than the thought of gaining something of the same value. Salespeople use it constantly: only a few left, or the offer ends on Friday.",
    "Part of the effect is a shortcut, since things that are hard to get usually are better. Part of it is what psychologists call reactance: when our freedom to have something is limited, we want it more. It appears in two-year-olds and in teenagers forbidden to see someone, and Cialdini notes research suggesting that parents who oppose a young couple's romance can deepen it. When Miami banned detergents containing phosphates, residents drove to other counties to stock up and judged the banned detergents to work better than before.",
    "Scarcity works best when something becomes newly scarce, and when we must compete for it. In an experiment, people rated cookies from a jar of two as more desirable than identical cookies from a jar of ten, and rated them higher still when the jar had just gone from ten to two. A beef importer told some of his customers that a shortage of Australian beef was coming; they bought more than twice as much as customers who heard only the usual sales pitch. Those also told that the news came from an exclusive source bought six times as much.",
    "Cialdini closes the book by defending the shortcuts themselves. Life moves too fast for anyone to think through every decision, and these rules are mostly reliable. The danger is people who fake the signals: the invented deadline, the paid testimonial, the actor in a white coat. His advice is to stay alert to the rush of feeling that a shortcut produces, and to remember that the thing we want is worth what it is worth, scarce or not. The cookies from the nearly empty jar, he reminds us, did not taste any better."
   ]
  }
 ],
 "sources": [
  "Robert B. Cialdini, Influence: The Psychology of Persuasion, revised edition (Harper Business, 2006), ISBN 9780061899874, https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780061899874"
 ]
}
''',
  'summaries/sapiens.json?v=72ca179a1108': r'''
{
 "format": 1,
 "book": "sapiens",
 "title": "Sapiens",
 "author": "Yuval Noah Harari",
 "year": 2014,
 "language": "en",
 "words": 2190,
 "minutes": 15,
 "parts": [
  {
   "title": "One human species among several",
   "text": [
    "Yuval Noah Harari, a historian at the Hebrew University of Jerusalem, tells the whole human story in one book, organised around revolutions. The Cognitive Revolution, about 70,000 years ago, gave our species its peculiar mind. The Agricultural Revolution, about 12,000 years ago, tied it to fields and villages. The Scientific Revolution, which began about 500 years ago, may end it, by giving it the power to remake itself.",
    "He starts by taking us down a peg. Humans first evolved in East Africa about 2.5 million years ago, and for most of that time they were unremarkable animals somewhere in the middle of the food chain. They spread out of Africa across Europe and Asia, and in different places evolved into different species. A hundred thousand years ago the earth was home to at least six kinds of human: Neanderthals in Europe and western Asia, Homo erectus in eastern Asia, the small people of the island of Flores, and others. We are used to being the only humans, and Harari treats that as the strange part.",
    "Our ancestors had paid for their large brains. In Homo sapiens the brain is about 2 to 3 per cent of body weight and uses about a quarter of the body's energy at rest. Walking upright freed the hands but narrowed the hips, so babies are born early and helpless and need years of care, which made humans intensely social. Fire, used daily by about 300,000 years ago, let them cook, and cooking let them digest more food with smaller guts.",
    "Sapiens itself evolved in East Africa, and around 70,000 years ago it began spreading across the world. The other humans then vanished. Harari weighs two theories: that Sapiens interbred with the others, or replaced them. The genetic evidence points to some of each. About 1 to 4 per cent of the DNA that sets modern people of the Middle East and Europe apart is Neanderthal. But the other species did disappear, and Harari suspects Sapiens had a hand in it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Stories that let strangers cooperate",
   "text": [
    "What made Sapiens different? Harari's answer is language, and one feature of it in particular. Many animals can warn of a lion. Sapiens could also talk about each other, and gossip let groups keep track of who was trustworthy. But the truly unusual thing was the ability to talk about things that do not exist at all: spirits, gods, nations, laws. You could never persuade a monkey to give you a banana by promising it unlimited bananas after death, in monkey heaven.",
    "Fiction matters because it lets large numbers of strangers cooperate. Chimpanzees live in troops of a few dozen. Human groups held together by personal acquaintance top out at about 150 people, the most anyone can know well. Beyond that, cooperation rests on shared myths. Two Catholics who have never met can work together on a crusade or a hospital, because both believe in the same God. Two lawyers who have never met can defend a stranger, because both believe in laws and human rights.",
    "Harari's example is Peugeot, the car company, named after the family that founded it. You cannot point to Peugeot in its cars or its factories. If every employee died and every factory burned, the company could borrow money and rebuild. If a judge ordered it dissolved, the factories would still stand, but Peugeot would be gone. It exists because lawyers performed the right rituals and enough people believe in it, a kind of legal fiction called a limited liability company.",
    "Harari calls these shared fictions imagined realities, and he insists they are real in their effects. They also gave Sapiens a way to change its behaviour quickly. Other social animals mostly behave as their genes dictate, and change only as their genes change. Sapiens could change by changing its stories: the French went almost overnight, in 1789, from believing in the divine right of kings to believing in the sovereignty of the people."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Foragers, and the animals they wiped out",
   "text": [
    "For most of their history Sapiens lived as foragers, and Harari argues that our minds are still shaped by that life. Our craving for sweet, fatty food made sense to a forager who found a tree heavy with ripe figs and ate as many as possible before the baboons arrived. In a world of supermarkets the same urge makes us fat.",
    "He paints forager life as better than we usually imagine. Foragers ate dozens of different foods, which protected them from famine when one failed. They had fewer infectious diseases, because most of those come from domesticated animals and crowded settlements. Many worked fewer hours than farmers would. Their lives had real dangers, including high child mortality and violence, and Harari does not pretend otherwise. But the average forager probably knew more about the surrounding world than most of us do.",
    "Foragers were also devastating. Sapiens reached Australia about 45,000 years ago, a journey that meant crossing open sea. Within a few thousand years, 23 of the 24 Australian animal species weighing 100 pounds or more were extinct, among them giant kangaroos and a marsupial lion. Climate may have played a part, but Harari notes that Australia's animals had survived earlier climate shifts. What was new was us, hunting animals that had not evolved to fear humans, and burning forests to clear them.",
    "The same thing happened in the Americas, which Sapiens reached about 16,000 years ago, and on island after island across the oceans. Mammoths and giant ground sloths disappeared soon after humans arrived, along with many other large animals. Long before anyone wrote a word, Harari concludes, our species had driven to extinction about half of the planet's big land animals. The romantic picture of ancient peoples living in harmony with nature does not survive these numbers."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "History's biggest fraud",
   "text": [
    "Between about 9500 and 8500 BC, in the hill country of the Middle East, Sapiens began to spend nearly all its time tending a few species of plants and animals: wheat, lentils, goats. Within a few thousand years farming had spread across much of the world. The textbook story is that clever humans made their lives easier. Harari calls the Agricultural Revolution history's biggest fraud.",
    "Farmers worked harder than foragers, ate a poorer diet built on one or two crops, and suffered more disease. Their skeletons show slipped discs and arthritis from long days of digging and carrying. A drought or a blight could mean famine. Looking at it from the plant's side, Harari puts it in one of his most quoted lines: \"We did not domesticate wheat. It domesticated us.\" A grass that grew in a small corner of the Middle East now covers vast areas of the planet, tended by humans who clear its fields and carry its water.",
    "Why did anyone accept the deal? Because it came in small steps, each of which seemed sensible. Farming fed more people per acre, so populations grew, and once a village had more children there was no going back to foraging. Each generation had more people living less well. Harari calls this the luxury trap: a gain meant to make life easier becomes a necessity that makes it harder, as email has done for many office workers.",
    "There may have been more to it than food. At Göbekli Tepe in Turkey, people who still foraged built monumental stone pillars around 9500 BC. Harari suggests that such shared projects might have pushed people to settle and grow wheat, rather than the other way round: first the temple, then the village. The domesticated animals paid the heaviest price. In evolutionary terms chickens and cattle are huge successes, but Harari counts them among the most miserable creatures that ever lived."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Orders that exist only in our heads",
   "text": [
    "Farming produced surpluses, and surpluses fed kings, priests, soldiers and the first cities. Keeping millions of people working together required imagined orders. Harari compares the Code of Hammurabi, written in Babylon around 1776 BC, with the American Declaration of Independence of 1776. The first claims that people are born unequal and sets different penalties according to class. The second claims that all men are created equal. Both, Harari says, are imagined orders. Neither describes anything in our biology, and both work only because people believe them.",
    "Large societies also needed to remember far more than a brain can hold. The Sumerians invented writing, between 3500 and 3000 BC, to keep accounts of taxes and debts. One of the earliest names we can read belongs to an accountant: a clay tablet from the city of Uruk records grain received over thirty-seven months and appears to bear the signature Kushim. Writing began as bookkeeping, and poetry came later.",
    "Then there is money, which Harari calls the most universal system of mutual trust ever invented. The Sumerians paid with fixed measures of barley, and later with the shekel, a set weight of silver. Coins and numbers on a screen work for the same reason barley did: everyone believes everyone else will accept them. Christians and Muslims who could not agree on God happily accepted each other's gold.",
    "Money, empires and universal religions, in Harari's telling, pulled humanity toward a single global order. He is willing to defend empires, the most common form of government for the past 2,500 years. In the second century BC the Romans besieged and destroyed the Spanish town of Numantia. Centuries later, Spaniards celebrated Numantia as a symbol of national courage, in Spanish, a language descended from Latin, as Catholics, under laws based on Roman ones."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Admitting ignorance, then conquering",
   "text": [
    "The Scientific Revolution, Harari argues, began with a confession. Earlier traditions of knowledge assumed that everything important was already known, by God or by the ancients. Modern science starts from the idea that we do not know, and that what we think we know could be proved wrong. Around 1500, European mapmakers began drawing world maps with empty spaces on them, admitting that there were lands still unknown. That admission sent ships out to fill them.",
    "Science on its own does not explain why Europe came to dominate the world. Harari's answer is that science joined forces with empire and with capitalism. Each fed the others. When James Cook sailed for the Pacific in 1768, his ship carried astronomers to observe the transit of Venus, and botanists and artists to record new plants and peoples. The voyage advanced knowledge, and it also prepared the way for the British occupation of Australia. When Napoleon invaded Egypt in 1798 he took 165 scholars with him.",
    "Capitalism, in turn, rests on a new kind of trust in the future. For most of history the economy hardly grew, so lending was risky and credit scarce. Once people believed the pie would keep growing, they were willing to lend against tomorrow's profits, and the profits paid for more discoveries. Harari's illustration is a fund set up in 1744 by two Scottish ministers, Alexander Webster and Robert Wallace, to support the widows of clergymen. Using the new mathematics of probability, they forecast what the fund would hold in 1765, more than twenty years ahead, and their figure was right almost to the pound.",
    "The darker side of the bargain was force. In 1840 Britain went to war with China to defend the trade of British merchants selling opium there. Belief in growth, Harari observes, did not come with any promise about who would pay for it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Are we any happier",
   "text": [
    "After all these revolutions, Harari asks the question historians usually skip: are people happier than they were? Material progress is plain to see. Famine and plague kill far fewer people than they once did, and since 1945 wars between great powers have become rare. Yet happiness depends on expectations, and expectations rise with conditions. Comforts that would have delighted our ancestors become the minimum we expect, so feeling better off is harder than being better off.",
    "Biologists offer another answer: happiness is a matter of chemistry, of serotonin and dopamine, and our brains evolved to give us brief rewards that fade, so that we keep striving. Harari also considers the view that happiness lies in seeing meaning in one's life, whether or not that meaning is a story we tell ourselves. He cites Daniel Kahneman's finding that parents rate the moments of raising children as mostly drudgery, and yet count their children as their greatest source of happiness. And he gives space to the Buddhist position, that suffering comes from craving pleasant feelings, and that the way out is to stop chasing them.",
    "The book ends looking forward. Sapiens is beginning to break the laws of natural selection and replace them with intelligent design. Scientists already engineer bacteria and animals, and are working to connect brains to machines and to extend human life far beyond its natural span. Harari suggests that our descendants may become a different kind of being altogether.",
    "His last pages put the challenge in a question. We have gained the powers of gods, yet we remain unsure of our goals and unaccountable to anyone. Harari's closing warning is that nothing is more dangerous than gods who are dissatisfied and irresponsible and do not know what they want. The question for the future, as he frames it, is less what we can become than what we want to want."
   ]
  }
 ],
 "sources": [
  "Yuval Noah Harari, Sapiens: A Brief History of Humankind (Harper, 2015), ISBN 9780062316097, https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780062316097"
 ]
}
''',
  'summaries/the-structure-of-scientific-revolutions.json?v=b15db14d48af': r'''
{
 "format": 1,
 "book": "the-structure-of-scientific-revolutions",
 "title": "The Structure of Scientific Revolutions",
 "author": "Thomas S. Kuhn",
 "year": 1962,
 "language": "en",
 "words": 2180,
 "minutes": 15,
 "parts": [
  {
   "title": "Science that moves in jumps",
   "text": [
    "Thomas Kuhn trained as a physicist. While he was still a graduate student at Harvard, he was drawn into an experimental course that taught physical science to students who were not scientists, and it was his first real encounter with the history of science. Reading the old texts closely unsettled what he believed about how science works. The Structure of Scientific Revolutions, published in 1962, was his attempt to draw a truer picture. It first appeared as a volume of the International Encyclopedia of Unified Science, a series edited by philosophers who saw science as the steady growth of tested knowledge, which is close to the picture the book took apart.",
    "The picture he wanted to replace is the one in science textbooks. There, knowledge grows by accumulation: each generation adds its facts to the pile and hands on a larger, truer store. Kuhn argues that this is a story told after the fact. Read the record as it happened and you find long quiet stretches in which a field shares one way of working, broken by short upheavals in which that way of working is thrown out and replaced. He called the quiet stretches normal science and the upheavals scientific revolutions.",
    "The key idea came to him in an unexpected place. Kuhn spent 1958 and 1959 at a centre for the behavioural sciences at Stanford, surrounded by social scientists, and was struck by how openly they disagreed about which problems mattered and which methods were legitimate. Natural scientists seldom argue about such basics. Trying to explain the difference, he arrived at the notion that made the book famous: the paradigm.",
    "The word has since left the book. People talk about a paradigm shift when a company changes its strategy. Kuhn meant something narrower and stranger, and the argument behind it changed how historians and philosophers talk about science, and how many scientists talk about themselves."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "What a paradigm is",
   "text": [
    "Kuhn starts in the time before anyone agrees. In the first half of the eighteenth century, he notes, there were almost as many views of electricity as there were people experimenting with it. One group treated attraction as the basic effect, another gave repulsion equal weight, and others pictured electricity as a fluid running through conductors. Each school explained some of the facts and had to wave others away. In periods like this, Kuhn writes of optics before Newton, every writer had to build the field anew from its foundations.",
    "Then Benjamin Franklin and his followers offered an account that explained most of the known effects at once, including the puzzling Leyden jar, a glass vessel that could store a charge and give a shock. Freed from arguing about everything, the people who adopted it could study chosen effects in far more detail, and the rival schools faded as their followers went over.",
    "Kuhn calls achievements of this kind paradigms, and he lists several: Aristotle's Physics, Ptolemy's Almagest, Newton's Principia and Opticks, Franklin's Electricity, Lavoisier's Chemistry and Lyell's Geology. Each did two things. It was impressive enough to draw a lasting group of followers away from competing ways of doing science, and it was open-ended enough to leave them plenty of problems to solve.",
    "A paradigm includes a theory, and it also sets the example of how good work is done: which questions count, and what an acceptable answer looks like. Students absorb it from textbooks and by solving the problems their teachers solved. That is why a mature science can move so fast. Nobody has to defend first principles in every paper, and researchers can go deep into details that no one would bother with unless they trusted the framework."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Normal science solves puzzles",
   "text": [
    "Most scientists, most of the time, do what Kuhn calls normal science, and he describes it with deliberate plainness as mopping up. Mopping up, he writes, is what occupies most scientists for their entire careers. A paradigm promises that the world works in a certain way, and normal science works out that promise in detail. It measures the quantities the theory says are important, more and more precisely. It builds apparatus to show theory and nature agreeing, like Atwood's machine, designed almost a century after Newton's Principia to show his second law at work. And it extends the theory to cases it has not yet covered.",
    "Kuhn compares this work to solving puzzles, in the sense of a crossword or a jigsaw. A puzzle is guaranteed to have a solution, and there are rules about what counts as one. The researcher usually knows roughly what the answer should be. The challenge, and the satisfaction, is in finding a way to get there. Normal science, he writes, does not aim at new kinds of fact or theory, and when it succeeds it finds none.",
    "This has a consequence that sounds unscientific and turns out to be essential. Inside normal science the paradigm itself is not on trial. When a puzzle resists, the failure reflects on the researcher and leaves the theory alone. Kuhn quotes the proverb that it is a poor carpenter who blames his tools.",
    "He defends this stubbornness. Because the paradigm is not given up easily, scientists are not distracted by every odd result, and the problems that finally do force a change have to cut deep into what the field knows. A community that dropped its framework at the first contrary measurement would never push it far enough to find out exactly where it fails."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "When nature refuses to fit",
   "text": [
    "Normal science is not designed to find novelties, and yet it keeps finding them. Kuhn's explanation is that discovery begins with an anomaly: a result the paradigm says should not happen. Only someone who knows precisely what to expect can notice that something is wrong, so the narrow, detailed work of normal science is what makes surprises visible.",
    "His examples show how untidy discovery is. Ask who discovered oxygen and there is no clean answer. The Swedish chemist Carl Scheele had prepared the gas, though his account appeared later. Joseph Priestley isolated it in 1774 and took it for air with less phlogiston in it, phlogiston being the substance chemists then believed was given off in burning. Antoine Lavoisier saw that it was a new gas, one of the main parts of the atmosphere, and built a new chemistry on it. Discovering something truly new, Kuhn concludes, takes time, and a change in what the discoverers believe.",
    "X-rays arrived in a similar way. In 1895 Wilhelm Roentgen noticed a screen glowing across his laboratory when nothing should have made it glow. No theory forbade such rays, but they broke expectations so deeply rooted that they were met with surprise and some shock. Lord Kelvin at first called them an elaborate hoax.",
    "To show why the new is hard to see, Kuhn borrows an experiment by the psychologists Jerome Bruner and Leo Postman. They flashed playing cards at people and slipped in impossible ones, such as a red six of spades or a black four of hearts. At short exposures people saw them as normal cards, without hesitation. With longer looks many grew uneasy, and some became distressed. One said he was no longer sure what a spade looked like. Even when the cards stayed in view far longer than needed for normal ones, more than one in ten of the odd cards went unrecognised. Scientists, Kuhn suggests, see in much the same way: novelty emerges only with difficulty, against a background of expectation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Crisis, and why theories survive it",
   "text": [
    "A single anomaly rarely brings a paradigm down. Every theory lives with results it cannot explain, and scientists set them aside as problems for later. For decades after Newton, calculations of the moon's motion failed to match one feature astronomers measured, the slow turning of its orbit. Leading mathematicians proposed changing the law of gravity itself. Then Alexis Clairaut found that the fault lay in the mathematics, and Newton's law stood.",
    "Crisis comes when anomalies pile up and the best people in a field begin to doubt the rules themselves. Kuhn's main example is astronomy before Copernicus. Ptolemy's system of circles upon circles had been adjusted for centuries to follow the planets, and a discrepancy fixed in one place tended to show up in another. By the sixteenth century the result was so complicated that Copernicus compared it to a monster, a figure assembled from hands, feet and a head taken from different bodies.",
    "Other revolutions also began in crisis. Chemists who believed in phlogiston struggled to explain why metals grew heavier when they were roasted, if burning meant losing something. At the end of the nineteenth century, physicists could not detect the earth's motion through the ether, the medium that was supposed to carry light. Lavoisier's chemistry and Einstein's relativity grew out of those failures.",
    "Even in crisis, scientists do not simply abandon their paradigm. Kuhn insists that a theory is declared invalid only when a rival is ready to take its place, because to reject one paradigm without putting another in its place would be to stop doing science. Instead the rules loosen. Versions of the theory multiply, and researchers try ideas they would have dismissed in calmer times. As in manufacture, Kuhn writes, retooling is an extravagance kept for the occasion that demands it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "A different world after the revolution",
   "text": [
    "When a new paradigm wins, Kuhn argues, the change goes far deeper than swapping one theory for another. Scientists come to see the world differently. He compares it to the familiar drawing that is a duck one moment and a rabbit the next: what were ducks in the scientist's world before the revolution are rabbits afterwards.",
    "His examples are among the most memorable in the book. Watching a heavy stone swing on a string, followers of Aristotle saw a body falling with difficulty. Galileo saw a pendulum, a motion that almost repeats itself forever. Within half a century of Copernicus, Western astronomers began to record new stars and other changes in heavens they had believed unchanging. Chinese astronomers, whose ideas about the cosmos allowed for change, had recorded such things for centuries. Priestley went on seeing dephlogisticated air where Lavoisier saw oxygen, and never accepted the new chemistry.",
    "This is why debates between paradigms are so hard to settle. Each side argues from inside its own framework, and the same words carry different meanings on each side. Mass in Newton's physics is conserved, while in Einstein's it can be converted into energy. Newton's laws can be recovered from Einstein's as a special case only by quietly changing what the terms mean. Kuhn's word for rival traditions like these is incommensurable: there is no neutral measure against which both can be judged.",
    "A change of allegiance is therefore, in Kuhn's word, a conversion, and no proof can force it. He quotes Max Planck's bleak remark that a new scientific truth triumphs as its opponents eventually die and a generation grows up familiar with it. He also quotes Darwin, who at the end of the Origin of Species placed his hopes in young and rising naturalists. Arguments still matter. A new paradigm wins people over by solving the problems that broke the old one, and sometimes simply by being neater. But no single experiment converts everyone at once."
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Progress without a destination",
   "text": [
    "If revolutions are this disruptive, why does science look like a smooth climb? Kuhn's answer is that revolutions become invisible. After each one the textbooks are rewritten, and the history in them is rewritten too, so that earlier scientists seem to have worked on today's problems with today's methods. Students inherit a tidy line of progress, and the questions of the losing side drop out of view.",
    "Does science progress at all, then? Kuhn says it does, and changes what progress means. A mature science gets steadily better at solving its puzzles, and a new paradigm has to keep most of the problem-solving power of the old one while adding more. What he doubts is that each change brings scientists closer to a final truth about nature. He compares science to evolution as Darwin described it, a process that moves away from primitive beginnings without being drawn toward any goal.",
    "Critics accused him of making science irrational, a matter of persuasion and fashion, and of using his central word loosely. On the second charge he largely agreed. In a postscript added in 1969 he reports that one sympathetic reader had found paradigm used in at least twenty-two different ways. He split the word in two: the shared beliefs and values of a scientific community, which he called its disciplinary matrix, and the concrete solved problems that students learn from, which he called exemplars.",
    "To the charge of irrationality he answered that scientists do choose between theories for reasons, such as accuracy of prediction and simplicity. Those values are shared, but they do not point everyone to the same answer at the same moment, which is why a new paradigm spreads person by person until a community has turned.",
    "Read today, the book describes how any community of experts works. Its shared assumptions are what let it work fast, and they also decide which surprises its members are able to see."
   ]
  }
 ],
 "sources": [
  "Thomas S. Kuhn, The Structure of Scientific Revolutions, 50th anniversary edition, with an introductory essay by Ian Hacking (University of Chicago Press, 2012), ISBN 9780226458120, https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780226458120"
 ]
}
''',
};
